d "3be53353-1576-5fe4-8702-a592e7a153f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186154163 #edb/ref 1080 "2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186154163 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186154163 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d302a617-4235-5398-b3ba-b9139919e6b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186154164 #edb/ref 1080 "2a + 1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186154164 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186154164 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1ed2b28a-549b-559b-bb0d-83d5abc01fba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186154165 #edb/ref 1080 "2^{a + 1}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186154165 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186154165 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5e1ecb9a-cf2e-5b81-9035-45b5711bad9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157013 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13301" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157013 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we wish to construct a direct proof of the following statement:\n\n> $5n+4$ *is odd if* $n$ *is odd.*\n\nA proof schema is outlined below. Fill in the missing entries.\n\n> Let $n$ be an $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid 1pt]{\\phantom{\\textrm{odd}}}$ integer. Then, $n=\\bbox[3pt,border:solid 1pt]{\\phantom{2a+1}}$ for some integer $a$.\n> Substituting $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid 1pt]{\\phantom{n=2a+1}}$ into the expression $5n+4$, we can show that $5n+4=\\bbox[3pt,border:solid 1pt]{\\phantom{2b+1}}$, where $b$ is $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid 1pt]{\\phantom{\\textrm{an integer}}}$.\n> Therefore, since $5n+4$ $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid 1pt]{\\phantom{\\textrm{is one more than}}}$ a multiple of $2$, we conclude that $5n+4$ is $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid 1pt]{\\phantom{\\textrm{odd}}}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157013 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f54c74aa-3a36-52a3-ba40-d5f995fbeb77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157013 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p$.\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1$.\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n\n$$\nn \\text{is odd} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 5n+4 \\text{is odd}\n$$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is odd.\n\n> *Let $n$ be an $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{odd}}$ integer. Then, $n=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2a+1}$ for some integer $a$.*\n\nThe idea is to substitute\n\n$$\nn=2a+1\n$$\n\ninto our expression $5n+4$ and show it is *odd* by writing it as *one more than* a multiple of $2$.\n\n> *Substituting $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}n=2a+1}$ into the expression $5n+4$, we can show that $5n+4=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2b+1}$, where $b$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{an integer}}$.*\n\nOnce we've shown this, the proof is complete, and we can state our conclusion.\n\n> *Therefore, since $5n+4$ $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{is one more than}}$ a multiple of $2$, we conclude that $5n+4$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{odd}}$.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157014 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13307" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157014 #edb/ref 1071 "Prove that $15n+7$ is odd whenever $n$ is even." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157014 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ff0076b9-83aa-500e-8501-7eb9b4e6889d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157014 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p$.\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1$.\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n$n$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is even.\n\n> Let $n$ be an even integer. Then, $n=2a$ for some integer $a$.\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a$ into our expression $15n+7$ and show it is odd by writing it as a multiple of $2$, plus $1$.\n\n> Therefore,\n> $15n + 7|= 15(2a) + 7; = 30a + 7; = (30a + 6) + 1; = 2(15a + 3)_(⏟)_(b) + 1$.\n\nNow, if we define another integer\n\n$$\nb = 15a+3\n$$\n\n(highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\n15n+7 = 2b+1\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $15n+7$ is indeed one more than a multiple of $2$.\n\n> Let $b = 15a+3$. Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n> Therefore, we have\n> $15n + 7|= 2(15a + 3)_(⏟)_(b) + 1; = 2b + 1$\n> which is one larger than a multiple of $2$.\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\n> Therefore, $15n+7$ is odd." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157054 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6877" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157054 #edb/ref 1071 "Prove that if $n$ is odd, then $7n-1$ is even." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157054 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "296186e1-8e54-5697-86ad-fe52356f4b4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157054 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p$.\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1$.\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n$n$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is odd.\n\n> If $n$ is odd, then $n=2a+1$ for some integer $a$.\n\nThe idea is to substitute\n\n$$\nn=2a+1\n$$\n\ninto the expression $7n-1$ and show it is even by writing it as a multiple of $2$.\n\n> Therefore,\n> $7n - 1|= 7(2a + 1) - 1; = 14a + 7 - 1; = 14a + 6; = 2(7a + 3)_(⏟)_(b)$.\n\nNow, if we define another integer\n\n$$\nb=7a+3\n$$\n\n(highlighted above), we have that $7n-1 = 2b$, which shows that $7n-1$ is indeed a multiple of $2$.\n\n> Let $b = 7a+3$. Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n> Therefore, we have\n> $7n - 1|= 2(7a + 3)_(⏟)_(b); = 2b$\n> which is a multiple of $2$.\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\n> Therefore, $7n-1$ is even." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157055 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6882" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157055 #edb/ref 1071 "Prove that $n^2 - 2$ is odd if $n$ is odd." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157055 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1cb515bc-5e40-577e-9714-1e4537974b62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157055 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p$.\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1$.\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n$n$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is odd.\n\n> Since $n$ is odd, it can be expressed as $n=2a+1$ for some integer $a$.\n\nThe idea is to substitute\n\n$$\nn=2a+1\n$$\n\ninto our expression $n^2 - 2$ and show it is odd by writing it as a multiple of $2$, plus $1$.\n\n> Now, writing $n^2 - 2$ in terms of $a$, we obtain the following:\n> $n^{2} - 2|= (2a + 1)^{2} - 2; = (4a^{2} + 4a + 1) - 2; = (4a^{2} + 4a - 2) + 1; = 2(2a^{2} + 2a - 1)_(⏟)_(b) + 1$.\n\nNow, if we define another integer\n\n$$\nb=2a^2 + 2a - 1\n$$\n\n(highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\nn^2 - 2 = 2b+1\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $n^2 - 2$ is indeed one more than a multiple of $2$.\n\n> Let $b=2a^2 + 2a - 1$. Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n> Therefore, we have\n> $n^{2} - 2|= 2(2a^{2} + 2a - 1)_(⏟)_(b) + 1; = 2b + 1$\n> which is one more than a multiple of $2$.\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\n> Therefore, $n^2 - 2$ is odd." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157253 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157253 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157253 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157253 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157253 #edb/ref 1039 "Completing a Proof Schema" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157253 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157253 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e35ca10a-b9bf-5e11-bbe4-4ebe568cbb0a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157254 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186051079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157254 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157254 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759